% Origin: Kowalski & Calejo, "Teaching Logical Thinking through Logic
% Programming using Logical English, Argumentation Games and Animation",
% PEG Lisbon 2026, slide 24: "Abduction can be used to explain observations."
%
% The observation "the grass is wet" is the query/goal. The two candidate
% causes are open (abducible) predicates, declared "; assumable": since they
% cannot be proved, each is ASSUMED in order to make the goal true, and is
% returned as an unknown. So the query yields two alternative explanations,
% one per answer:
%     the grass is wet, assuming: it rained.
%     the grass is wet, assuming: the sprinkler was on.
% Read each answer together with its unknowns as a conditional answer
% (slide 26): "the grass is wet IF it rained".
%
% The beliefs here are genuinely propositional; the verifier no longer flags
% "rule without variables" when EVERY rule in a program is ground, so a wholly
% propositional KB like this one loads without that noise.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    the grass is wet.
    it rained; assumable.
    the sprinkler was on; assumable.

the knowledge base wet grass includes:

the grass is wet if it rained.

the grass is wet if the sprinkler was on.

scenario observation is:
    explain expects answers [
        "the grass is wet",
        "the grass is wet"
    ] and unknowns [
        "it rained",
        "the sprinkler was on"
    ].

query explain is:
    the grass is wet.
